00:04:03.337 --> 00:04:04.920
PARVEEN PATEL: In
the next few slides,

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:07.550
we will show you how
Google Cloud uses

00:04:07.550 --> 00:04:09.740
the power of
software-defined networking

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:14.150
to deliver high
performance and low latency

00:04:14.150 --> 00:04:16.610
on a general-purpose,
multi-tenant cloud

00:04:16.610 --> 00:04:18.160
infrastructure.

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:23.330
GCP supports up to 100 Gbps
of bandwidth on a single VM

00:04:23.330 --> 00:04:26.660
and delivers latency as
low as 10 microseconds

00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:31.140
between two VMs in the same
placement policy group.

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:33.350
So let's get started.

00:04:33.350 --> 00:04:36.600
Google was one of the pioneers
of software-defined networking

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:39.920
for both physical networks
and virtual networks.

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:45.340
Google's virtual networking
SDN stack is called Andromeda.

00:04:45.340 --> 00:04:48.390
A key aspect of the
SDN architecture

00:04:48.390 --> 00:04:52.170
is the separation of the control
plane from its data plane.

00:04:52.170 --> 00:04:54.980
The data plane resides
in physical switches,

00:04:54.980 --> 00:04:58.420
for example, the edge
routers and end data center,

00:04:58.420 --> 00:05:01.750
and virtual switches
like the hypervisor

00:05:01.750 --> 00:05:04.260
on every machine in the cloud.

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:06.570
A logically centralized
control plane

00:05:06.570 --> 00:05:09.000
has a complete
view of the network

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:11.250
as well as the customer intent.

00:05:11.250 --> 00:05:13.980
The controller then
realizes customer intent

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:17.130
across different
data plane elements.

00:05:17.130 --> 00:05:19.080
For example, the
Andromeda controller

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:23.790
shown here in this picture
takes a VPC firewall policy,

00:05:23.790 --> 00:05:27.300
translates it into appropriate
rules for each host that's

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:30.720
running a VM for
that VPC, and then

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:34.170
distributes that policy
to the virtual switches

00:05:34.170 --> 00:05:36.950
on those machines.

00:05:36.950 --> 00:05:39.080
An important benefit
of this architecture

00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:43.010
is that it scales better and
removes the bottlenecks found

00:05:43.010 --> 00:05:45.460
in traditional networking.

00:05:45.460 --> 00:05:48.940
For example, it lets us
provide the same VPC features

00:05:48.940 --> 00:05:52.990
to VMs, bare-metal
instances, and containers.

00:05:55.790 --> 00:05:58.030
The feature that
is most relevant

00:05:58.030 --> 00:06:02.260
to high-performance
computing is performance.

00:06:02.260 --> 00:06:03.730
And in particular,
the performance

00:06:03.730 --> 00:06:05.510
of the virtual switch
that implements

00:06:05.510 --> 00:06:10.580
most of cloud networking is
routing and security policies.

00:06:10.580 --> 00:06:14.540
So how does Google build
a fast virtual switch?

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:16.870
The Andromeda virtual
switch is built

00:06:16.870 --> 00:06:19.830
using a framework called Snap.

00:06:19.830 --> 00:06:23.730
Snap enables OS-bypass
packet processing pipelines

00:06:23.730 --> 00:06:25.590
and user mode.

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:29.070
Snaps are both running these
pipelines on dedicated threads

00:06:29.070 --> 00:06:31.810
or in shared threads.

00:06:31.810 --> 00:06:37.330
Andromeda on Snap has two
key components, a fast path

00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:42.580
and per-VM co-processors The
fast path packet pipeline

00:06:42.580 --> 00:06:47.440
runs on dedicated threads and
has a very small CPU budget,

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:51.175
literally nanoseconds
for every packet.

00:06:51.175 --> 00:06:54.460
More expensive pipelines
such as NAT and DoS

00:06:54.460 --> 00:06:59.050
protection-- they run
in per-VM co-processors.

00:06:59.050 --> 00:07:01.060
So this architecture
neatly enables

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:05.530
us to achieve both high
performance and isolation.

00:07:05.530 --> 00:07:08.440
That is one VM's traffic
does not affect another VM.

00:07:11.370 --> 00:07:14.220
Software tuning, such
as dedicated threads

00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:18.840
and optimizing the number of
instructions for a packet,

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:20.940
can only take you so far.

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:24.000
To get true HPC-class
performance,

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:28.870
hardware offloads have typically
played a critical role.

00:07:28.870 --> 00:07:32.890
Andromeda started out
its offload efforts

00:07:32.890 --> 00:07:36.010
with simpler offloads such
as TCP segmentation and then

00:07:36.010 --> 00:07:38.210
send/receive offloads.

00:07:38.210 --> 00:07:40.690
We then added encryption
offload to it,

00:07:40.690 --> 00:07:44.020
as it was one of the expensive
components of our packet

00:07:44.020 --> 00:07:46.060
processing pipeline.

00:07:46.060 --> 00:07:50.080
A recent enhancement which
is very relevant to HPC

00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:53.890
is the introduction of
zero-copy payload transfers

00:07:53.890 --> 00:07:57.970
between a VM's memory
and the NIC hardware.

00:07:57.970 --> 00:08:01.300
Andromeda still gets to
process headers of packets.

00:08:01.300 --> 00:08:05.410
And hence, it can continue to
enforce VPC policies and rate

00:08:05.410 --> 00:08:06.760
limits.

00:08:06.760 --> 00:08:08.320
But there is no
software involved

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:10.510
in the movement of
payload, which helps

00:08:10.510 --> 00:08:13.700
us achieve high performance.

00:08:13.700 --> 00:08:17.020
So now let's see how
well it works in practice

00:08:17.020 --> 00:08:19.690
and what performance
we are able to achieve.

00:08:19.690 --> 00:08:23.260
This graph here
shows how performance

00:08:23.260 --> 00:08:27.110
has been a big focus of
Google Cloud networking

00:08:27.110 --> 00:08:28.970
for many years.

00:08:28.970 --> 00:08:33.580
And we've been making continuous
improvements in performance.

00:08:33.580 --> 00:08:35.620
Only the last two
bars of this graph

00:08:35.620 --> 00:08:38.260
are relevant to current skews.

00:08:38.260 --> 00:08:43.510
We're able to hit line rate
of 100 Gbps inside your VMs.

00:08:43.510 --> 00:08:47.830
And this is available right
now on GPU-attached VMs.

00:08:47.830 --> 00:08:51.140
Other types of VMs
have up to 32 Gbps

00:08:51.140 --> 00:08:54.430
depending on the core count.

00:08:54.430 --> 00:08:57.390
So with that, we've solved
the bandwidth problem.

00:08:57.390 --> 00:09:02.010
What about the harder problem
of latency, which is also

00:09:02.010 --> 00:09:06.210
very critical to HPC workloads?

00:09:06.210 --> 00:09:11.940
For latency, the entire software
stack, as well as the network

00:09:11.940 --> 00:09:14.863
connecting the VMs
play a critical role.

00:09:14.863 --> 00:09:16.280
We've looked at
the virtual switch

00:09:16.280 --> 00:09:18.480
in the hypervisor at GCP.

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:22.510
Now let's take a look at
what's inside the guest VM

00:09:22.510 --> 00:09:25.860
and the rest of the HPC stack.

00:09:25.860 --> 00:09:30.720
In this figure, starting at the
very top, are the applications.

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:36.300
Tightly coupled MPI applications
are written to MPI frameworks.

00:09:36.300 --> 00:09:41.220
And GCP supports both
Intel MPI and Open MPI.

00:09:41.220 --> 00:09:45.180
These frameworks depend on
either directly the Linux

00:09:45.180 --> 00:09:48.090
TCP/IP stack, or they
access the networking

00:09:48.090 --> 00:09:51.990
stack through libraries
like the libfabric library.

00:09:51.990 --> 00:09:57.090
Now, Google has invented many
of the major improvements

00:09:57.090 --> 00:09:59.980
to TCP in the last few years.

00:09:59.980 --> 00:10:02.820
And we have one of the
best congestion control

00:10:02.820 --> 00:10:05.590
teams in the world.

00:10:05.590 --> 00:10:09.550
Underneath the TCP stack are
the drivers that interact

00:10:09.550 --> 00:10:11.650
with the virtualized NIC.

00:10:11.650 --> 00:10:15.340
GCP supports the
generic VirtIO driver.

00:10:15.340 --> 00:10:20.830
But recently, we also introduced
our own Google virtual NIC

00:10:20.830 --> 00:10:22.280
driver.

00:10:22.280 --> 00:10:26.360
This gVNIC device exposes the
best of Google's infrastructure

00:10:26.360 --> 00:10:28.080
to the guest OS.

00:10:28.080 --> 00:10:32.180
For example, the zero-copy
feature that I talked about

00:10:32.180 --> 00:10:34.730
is exposed by the gVNIC driver.

00:10:37.450 --> 00:10:39.940
Now, Google has one
of the best performing

00:10:39.940 --> 00:10:44.830
SDN-based physical networks
underneath Google Cloud.

00:10:44.830 --> 00:10:46.450
But no matter how
good the network is,

00:10:46.450 --> 00:10:50.230
one of the important things
you can do to reduce latency

00:10:50.230 --> 00:10:53.570
is really place VMs
next to each other.

00:10:53.570 --> 00:10:56.130
This is where the
placement policy comes in.

00:10:56.130 --> 00:11:00.070
GCP supports two types of
placement policies, compact

00:11:00.070 --> 00:11:01.840
and spread.

00:11:01.840 --> 00:11:05.350
VMs associated with the same
compact placement policy

00:11:05.350 --> 00:11:10.070
are placed very close to each
other in the data center.

00:11:10.070 --> 00:11:15.280
So now let's see how well
all this works in practice.

00:11:15.280 --> 00:11:19.300
The graph here
shows an experiment

00:11:19.300 --> 00:11:23.260
where we run the famous MPI
ping-pong benchmark on Google

00:11:23.260 --> 00:11:24.700
Cloud.

00:11:24.700 --> 00:11:28.600
The leftmost data point
is the baseline latency

00:11:28.600 --> 00:11:32.720
between two VMs without
any placement policy.

00:11:32.720 --> 00:11:37.270
And as you can see here, we
get about 28 microseconds.

00:11:37.270 --> 00:11:40.850
By choosing the right
VM type, in this case,

00:11:40.850 --> 00:11:45.280
the compute-optimized VMs that
Manoj will talk about in a bit,

00:11:45.280 --> 00:11:49.270
and associating them with
a compact placement policy,

00:11:49.270 --> 00:11:53.630
we were able to get the latency
down to 18 microseconds.

00:11:53.630 --> 00:11:56.320
So that's a good
10-microsecond improvement

00:11:56.320 --> 00:12:00.650
on the base latency in a zone.

00:12:00.650 --> 00:12:02.990
As we tune different
layers of the stack

00:12:02.990 --> 00:12:06.350
that I showed in
the last few slides,

00:12:06.350 --> 00:12:10.420
we see that latency
continued to improve.

00:12:10.420 --> 00:12:13.180
And the bottom
line is that we can

00:12:13.180 --> 00:12:17.650
get to under 10-microsecond
latency on Google Cloud today,

00:12:17.650 --> 00:12:20.620
which is sufficient for running
some of the most demanding

00:12:20.620 --> 00:12:22.330
workloads.

00:12:22.330 --> 00:12:24.340
Now, we don't
expect our customers

00:12:24.340 --> 00:12:27.190
to make all these
optimizations by hand.

00:12:27.190 --> 00:12:31.330
So we've created solutions
guide as well as bat script

00:12:31.330 --> 00:12:35.160
that you can run inside your
favorite VM instance types

00:12:35.160 --> 00:12:40.470
to activate these optimizations.

00:12:40.470 --> 00:12:42.070
So that gives you
a flavor of how

00:12:42.070 --> 00:12:47.210
GCP leverages SDN to build
the right network for HPC.

00:12:47.210 --> 00:12:50.800
This SDN-enabled network
gives you low latency and high

00:12:50.800 --> 00:12:52.490
throughput.

00:12:52.490 --> 00:12:55.670
Your MPI traffic can
leverage VPC features

00:12:55.670 --> 00:12:58.310
like the VPC firewall.

00:12:58.310 --> 00:13:01.760
And since all of this
is built via software

00:13:01.760 --> 00:13:04.130
on general-purpose
infrastructure,

00:13:04.130 --> 00:13:07.190
high-performance networking
is available on many instance

00:13:07.190 --> 00:13:13.760
types, both general purpose, as
well as compute-optimized VMs.

00:13:13.760 --> 00:13:18.905
Now let's take a look at compute
and storage infrastructure.

00:13:18.905 --> 00:13:21.280
MANOJ JAYADEVAN: Now that we
understand a little bit more

00:13:21.280 --> 00:13:25.310
about the networking magic that
connects everything together,

00:13:25.310 --> 00:13:26.950
let's talk a little
bit more about what

00:13:26.950 --> 00:13:29.170
other components of
the infrastructure

00:13:29.170 --> 00:13:31.690
is required to meet
HPC applications

00:13:31.690 --> 00:13:33.490
such as from Google Cloud.

00:13:33.490 --> 00:13:37.390
Google Cloud actually offers a
broad set of compute products

00:13:37.390 --> 00:13:40.350
that can suit all kinds of
applications starting from web

00:13:40.350 --> 00:13:43.520
servers and network
appliances, and of course,

00:13:43.520 --> 00:13:47.110
large-scale tightly coupled
MPI and [INAUDIBLE] workloads.

00:13:47.110 --> 00:13:51.280
The general purpose instance
is called N2 series or N2D

00:13:51.280 --> 00:13:52.240
machines.

00:13:52.240 --> 00:13:54.470
They actually balance
price and performance

00:13:54.470 --> 00:13:58.060
that are suitable for most
workloads that you may have.

00:13:58.060 --> 00:14:01.030
But we have specialized
compute-optimized VMs,

00:14:01.030 --> 00:14:05.460
called the C2 instances, that
offer high-end vCPU performance

00:14:05.460 --> 00:14:07.790
for HPC applications.

00:14:07.790 --> 00:14:10.540
We'll talk a little bit more
about the C2 instance type

00:14:10.540 --> 00:14:12.580
in the next slide.

00:14:12.580 --> 00:14:15.475
We also have a
memory-optimized VM type,

00:14:15.475 --> 00:14:17.410
called M2, that is
actually suitable

00:14:17.410 --> 00:14:21.120
for your memory-intensive
applications like I said,

00:14:21.120 --> 00:14:23.260
[INAUDIBLE].

00:14:23.260 --> 00:14:26.060
For lower TCU app-- again,
TCU is actually very, very

00:14:26.060 --> 00:14:29.350
important for HPC because,
as I mentioned before,

00:14:29.350 --> 00:14:32.540
a HPC infrastructure
can be quite expensive.

00:14:32.540 --> 00:14:35.120
So cost is very important
for a lot of customers.

00:14:35.120 --> 00:14:39.220
So for customers like that, we
offer the preemptible VM types.

00:14:39.220 --> 00:14:42.370
If your applications are
designed as a [INAUDIBLE]

00:14:42.370 --> 00:14:47.320
application and can withstand
possible preemptions,

00:14:47.320 --> 00:14:50.440
then the preemptible instance
type can save up to 80%

00:14:50.440 --> 00:14:52.340
of your time--

00:14:52.340 --> 00:14:53.710
of your savings.

00:14:53.710 --> 00:14:59.000
We also provide the best
accelerator actually in Cloud,

00:14:59.000 --> 00:15:02.810
including the NVIDIA GPUs and
Google innovations like tensor

00:15:02.810 --> 00:15:06.260
processing units to speed
up your applications

00:15:06.260 --> 00:15:07.925
and give you faster
time results.

00:15:10.500 --> 00:15:13.130
Now let's talk a little bit
about the compute-optimized VM

00:15:13.130 --> 00:15:15.200
or the C2 instance types.

00:15:15.200 --> 00:15:17.670
Compute-optimized VMs are well
suited for HPC application,

00:15:17.670 --> 00:15:20.330
as I said before, both
tightly coupled applications

00:15:20.330 --> 00:15:22.400
and high-throughput
applications.

00:15:22.400 --> 00:15:28.490
Compute-optimized instances
offer high-end vCPU performance

00:15:28.490 --> 00:15:30.340
for compute workloads
and offer up

00:15:30.340 --> 00:15:35.630
to 40% more performance compared
to your general purpose VMs.

00:15:35.630 --> 00:15:38.180
They also leverage the
second generation Intel Xeon

00:15:38.180 --> 00:15:40.730
processors and can run
at a sustained clock

00:15:40.730 --> 00:15:42.837
speed of about 3.8 gigahertz.

00:15:42.837 --> 00:15:45.170
Additionally-- this is actually
a very important point--

00:15:45.170 --> 00:15:48.560
C2 VMs can provide
full transparency

00:15:48.560 --> 00:15:51.140
to the architecture of the
underlying server platforms.

00:15:51.140 --> 00:15:54.140
The NUMA topology is actually
exposed to the guest operating

00:15:54.140 --> 00:15:54.950
system.

00:15:54.950 --> 00:15:57.970
And then applications can
actually make smart choices

00:15:57.970 --> 00:15:59.420
to use the NUMA topology.

00:15:59.420 --> 00:16:02.930
This actually improves
the performance a lot.

00:16:02.930 --> 00:16:08.810
You can choose a C2 instance up
to 60 vCPUs and 250 gigabytes

00:16:08.810 --> 00:16:13.120
of memory and attach up to 3
terabytes of local storage.

00:16:13.120 --> 00:16:19.030
C2 instances can also
deliver up to 32 Gbps

00:16:19.030 --> 00:16:23.200
of [INAUDIBLE] throughput rate
for the Light CPU configuration

00:16:23.200 --> 00:16:23.700
type.

00:16:23.700 --> 00:16:25.760
And we have a very
strong roadmap

00:16:25.760 --> 00:16:27.800
to basically improve this.

00:16:27.800 --> 00:16:31.520
And you can actually expect
those high performance

00:16:31.520 --> 00:16:34.400
throughput instances
to be on C2 instances.

00:16:34.400 --> 00:16:37.580
What we are observing is HPC and
machine learning infrastructure

00:16:37.580 --> 00:16:39.140
are undergoing a convergence.

00:16:39.140 --> 00:16:42.170
We are actually seeing more and
more machine learning workloads

00:16:42.170 --> 00:16:44.330
now using the HPC platform.

00:16:44.330 --> 00:16:48.130
GPUs are hence becoming a
very integral part of HPC.

00:16:48.130 --> 00:16:49.880
GCP actually offers
high-performance GPUs

00:16:49.880 --> 00:16:51.890
on Google Cloud for
machine learning,

00:16:51.890 --> 00:16:56.030
scientific computation,
and 3D visualization.

00:16:56.030 --> 00:16:59.223
GPUs can actually be added
to your VM instance type.

00:16:59.223 --> 00:17:01.390
And these VM instance types
are highly configurable.

00:17:01.390 --> 00:17:04.829
You can attach storage,
memory, bandwidth.

00:17:04.829 --> 00:17:08.130
And it actually acts like
a regular VM for you.

00:17:08.130 --> 00:17:13.700
But you can attach it to eight
GPUs for these instance types.

00:17:13.700 --> 00:17:16.270
GPUs will also work
with the GKE clusters.

00:17:16.270 --> 00:17:17.520
And GPU will always foll--

00:17:17.520 --> 00:17:20.109
it will also work with
your preemptible VMs,

00:17:20.109 --> 00:17:24.047
which is actually a very
common use case for HPC and ML.

00:17:24.047 --> 00:17:26.339
Now, one very important point
that I would like to make

00:17:26.339 --> 00:17:28.464
is we have a really strong
partnership with NVIDIA.

00:17:28.464 --> 00:17:33.120
And that actually helps
us bring GPUs that--

00:17:33.120 --> 00:17:37.030
the latest and the greatest GPUs
that basically NVIDIA comes out

00:17:37.030 --> 00:17:40.350
with on our platform
as fast as we can.

00:17:40.350 --> 00:17:43.440
Today, we support
the Tesla platform.

00:17:43.440 --> 00:17:46.500
And in the future, we are--
in the very near future,

00:17:46.500 --> 00:17:52.090
will be supporting the
NVIDIA's Ampere platform.

00:17:52.090 --> 00:17:54.090
From a bandwidth
standpoint, we actually

00:17:54.090 --> 00:17:58.630
deliver up to 100 gigs on
certain GPU types today.

00:17:58.630 --> 00:18:00.140
And this is actually in beta.

00:18:00.140 --> 00:18:02.770
But we have a very, very
strong roadmap coming up.

00:18:02.770 --> 00:18:06.316
And you can expect that
the higher throughput--

00:18:06.316 --> 00:18:07.900
that feature is
very, very important

00:18:07.900 --> 00:18:09.630
for the HPC-- is
going to be showing up

00:18:09.630 --> 00:18:14.170
for these GPU machine
types in the near future.

00:18:14.170 --> 00:18:16.700
Now, cost is actually very
important for customers,

00:18:16.700 --> 00:18:17.950
as we mentioned before, right?

00:18:17.950 --> 00:18:20.980
So we have various programs
and various options

00:18:20.980 --> 00:18:22.660
that's available for customers.

00:18:22.660 --> 00:18:26.060
For example, with preemptible
VM types, as I mentioned before,

00:18:26.060 --> 00:18:29.090
you can actually save
up to 80% of your cost.

00:18:29.090 --> 00:18:33.280
We also offer strong,
deep discounts

00:18:33.280 --> 00:18:36.520
for committed and
sustained users.

00:18:36.520 --> 00:18:39.130
You could actually
save up 30% to 55%

00:18:39.130 --> 00:18:45.460
depending on how long you
are using the VM instances.

00:18:48.130 --> 00:18:51.440
Now let's talk a little bit
about the storage support

00:18:51.440 --> 00:18:52.100
on GCP.

00:18:52.100 --> 00:18:53.650
Storage is also very important.

00:18:53.650 --> 00:18:54.560
It's very complex.

00:18:54.560 --> 00:18:57.320
And as I mentioned
before, the requirements

00:18:57.320 --> 00:19:00.440
for HPC application's
actually quite broad.

00:19:00.440 --> 00:19:02.840
Storage requirements for HPCs--

00:19:02.840 --> 00:19:05.600
they're very similar to what
any other regular enterprise

00:19:05.600 --> 00:19:08.050
application, but with
some exceptions, right?

00:19:08.050 --> 00:19:11.000
Google Cloud Platform delivers
various storage offerings

00:19:11.000 --> 00:19:13.890
like SQL, NoSQL, blog and
blog, and what have you.

00:19:13.890 --> 00:19:15.530
It's a broad set of offerings.

00:19:15.530 --> 00:19:18.920
But for-- we have some offering
that very specifically designed

00:19:18.920 --> 00:19:19.550
for HPC.

00:19:19.550 --> 00:19:22.700
For example, we have object
stores and file stores

00:19:22.700 --> 00:19:25.350
and a [INAUDIBLE]
compliant [INAUDIBLE]

00:19:25.350 --> 00:19:30.590
file system that is very
widely used in the HPC space.

00:19:30.590 --> 00:19:33.890
But the very first step that
a customer normally takes

00:19:33.890 --> 00:19:37.385
is, how do I move my data from
on-premise to Google Cloud?

00:19:37.385 --> 00:19:41.120
So for that, we actually
offer a service called TSOD.

00:19:41.120 --> 00:19:44.510
Customers can use this service
to hydrate their Google Cloud

00:19:44.510 --> 00:19:48.440
storage systems from on-premise.

00:19:48.440 --> 00:19:52.650
GCS, or the Google Cloud
Storage system, is our--

00:19:52.650 --> 00:19:54.680
the Google Cloud's object store.

00:19:54.680 --> 00:19:57.950
And a lot of customers actually
use this as primary storage.

00:19:57.950 --> 00:20:01.670
And a lot-- the
other customers use

00:20:01.670 --> 00:20:06.530
this for archival
storage on Google Cloud.

00:20:06.530 --> 00:20:09.100
We also have a broad
set of [INAUDIBLE],,

00:20:09.100 --> 00:20:11.255
but Google-managed
and partner-managed.

00:20:13.930 --> 00:20:18.650
Cloud Filestore is
our NFS file system.

00:20:18.650 --> 00:20:23.420
And the capacity that we
offer today is about 60 TB.

00:20:23.420 --> 00:20:26.510
We have also announced
the Filestore High Scale

00:20:26.510 --> 00:20:30.620
offering, which can actually
scale up to 325 terabytes.

00:20:30.620 --> 00:20:35.060
Customers also have the option
to use EMC or NetApp if they

00:20:35.060 --> 00:20:37.630
choose that as their option.

00:20:37.630 --> 00:20:40.270
Now, HPC customers who
are running tightly

00:20:40.270 --> 00:20:42.910
coupled applications or
MPI applications-- they'd

00:20:42.910 --> 00:20:46.605
normally require a [INAUDIBLE]
compliant [INAUDIBLE] system.

00:20:46.605 --> 00:20:49.680
So GCP has partnered with
a company called DDN.

00:20:49.680 --> 00:20:51.820
And jointly, we basically
provide an offering

00:20:51.820 --> 00:20:53.470
on Google Marketplace.

00:20:53.470 --> 00:20:57.540
But they can actually use
Lustre as their file system

00:20:57.540 --> 00:20:59.170
that has very high
[INAUDIBLE] ops

00:20:59.170 --> 00:21:02.480
and scales horizontally
for the HPC needs.

00:21:02.480 --> 00:21:05.440
So to sum it up, customers
can deploy the rights

00:21:05.440 --> 00:21:08.150
to a system for the
main application

00:21:08.150 --> 00:21:10.820
and get the best possible TCO.

00:21:10.820 --> 00:21:13.660
Now let us see how all
these things come together

00:21:13.660 --> 00:21:14.920
in our next few slides.

00:21:17.757 --> 00:21:19.340
PARVEEN PATEL: So
now that we gave you

00:21:19.340 --> 00:21:21.590
a glimpse of the
building blocks needed

00:21:21.590 --> 00:21:24.740
to build an HPC solution,
we'll take a look

00:21:24.740 --> 00:21:28.430
at two specific workloads.

00:21:28.430 --> 00:21:32.120
The first workload is a
weather forecasting workload.

00:21:32.120 --> 00:21:36.620
It's a benchmark that many
customers run on premise

00:21:36.620 --> 00:21:41.000
on their state-of-the-art
dedicated InfiniBand clusters.

00:21:41.000 --> 00:21:43.820
The first line on this
graph shows the performance

00:21:43.820 --> 00:21:46.740
some customers get on premise--

00:21:46.740 --> 00:21:48.260
faster completion,
which is-- you

00:21:48.260 --> 00:21:51.500
know, lower value is
better in this case.

00:21:51.500 --> 00:21:54.900
The second line shows how
this workload performed

00:21:54.900 --> 00:21:58.940
on GCP without many
of the optimizations

00:21:58.940 --> 00:22:00.950
we talked about today.

00:22:00.950 --> 00:22:03.350
Once we applied
these optimizations

00:22:03.350 --> 00:22:06.740
that we discussed plus some
workload-specific tuning,

00:22:06.740 --> 00:22:09.680
we were able to not only
match the performance

00:22:09.680 --> 00:22:13.130
the customers get on
premise, but we did it

00:22:13.130 --> 00:22:16.570
with 30% fewer CPU cores.

00:22:16.570 --> 00:22:18.372
Now, there are
additional optimizations

00:22:18.372 --> 00:22:20.080
that we have not shown
in this graph that

00:22:20.080 --> 00:22:23.440
can bring down the
completion time even further.

00:22:23.440 --> 00:22:26.050
But you're not always
shooting for lowest

00:22:26.050 --> 00:22:28.780
latency, but the best
price/performance

00:22:28.780 --> 00:22:31.550
ratio for your workload.

00:22:31.550 --> 00:22:34.060
And this workload,
this benchmark,

00:22:34.060 --> 00:22:35.760
demonstrates that really well.

00:22:38.490 --> 00:22:42.370
Let's also look at
the LS-DYNA workload.

00:22:42.370 --> 00:22:45.590
This is a well-known workload
in the automotive industry

00:22:45.590 --> 00:22:48.130
that does crash simulations.

00:22:48.130 --> 00:22:51.085
This workload is
notoriously tricky to scale.

00:22:51.085 --> 00:22:54.760
The two graphs here show
two different configurations

00:22:54.760 --> 00:22:57.160
of this workload.

00:22:57.160 --> 00:23:00.760
As you can see that with the
help of the optimizations

00:23:00.760 --> 00:23:03.580
that we discussed
today, this workload

00:23:03.580 --> 00:23:10.090
scales quite well up to multiple
hundreds of cores on GCP.

00:23:10.090 --> 00:23:11.830
This is as good as
what our customers are

00:23:11.830 --> 00:23:15.070
able to get on premise on
a dedicated, specialized

00:23:15.070 --> 00:23:17.290
infrastructure.

00:23:17.290 --> 00:23:19.360
We looked at many
such workloads.

00:23:19.360 --> 00:23:22.750
We only discussed these two.

00:23:22.750 --> 00:23:26.340
And what we find is that for
each workload and a given data

00:23:26.340 --> 00:23:29.080
set, there is a
sweet spot in terms

00:23:29.080 --> 00:23:31.570
of price/performance ratio.

00:23:31.570 --> 00:23:33.650
Once you have the
right set of tools,

00:23:33.650 --> 00:23:36.970
you can easily find that
sweet spot and provision

00:23:36.970 --> 00:23:40.000
and pay for just
enough infrastructure

00:23:40.000 --> 00:23:41.400
for your workload.

00:23:41.400 --> 00:23:45.400
And Cloud lets you make
that trade-off which was not

00:23:45.400 --> 00:23:49.160
even possible on premise.

00:23:49.160 --> 00:23:52.130
So in summary, we
would like to leave you

00:23:52.130 --> 00:23:53.990
with three key messages.

00:23:53.990 --> 00:23:56.270
You know, one is
that you no longer

00:23:56.270 --> 00:24:00.710
need to pay for a dedicated,
specialized infrastructure

00:24:00.710 --> 00:24:03.110
to run HPC workloads.

00:24:03.110 --> 00:24:05.540
Google Cloud makes
available to you

00:24:05.540 --> 00:24:08.320
the best-in-class
infrastructure and we

00:24:08.320 --> 00:24:11.840
use to run our own
high performance and AI

00:24:11.840 --> 00:24:14.350
machine learning workloads.

00:24:14.350 --> 00:24:18.370
Anyone can get access to
this infrastructure on demand

00:24:18.370 --> 00:24:22.280
and pay only for what they use.

00:24:22.280 --> 00:24:26.500
Number 2, HPC is a huge
area of investment for us.

00:24:26.500 --> 00:24:31.060
We built a number of solutions
for various industry verticals,

00:24:31.060 --> 00:24:35.030
and new ones are being
added to it every day.

00:24:35.030 --> 00:24:37.610
Number 3, if you don't see
your workload listed here,

00:24:37.610 --> 00:24:39.830
please do let us know.

00:24:39.830 --> 00:24:41.980
We want to make sure
that Google Cloud is

00:24:41.980 --> 00:24:45.800
the best at running all kinds
of high-performance computing,

00:24:45.800 --> 00:24:50.260
as well as high-performance
data analytics workload.

00:24:50.260 --> 00:24:51.280
Thank you.

00:24:51.280 --> 00:24:52.940
MANOJ JAYADEVAN: Thank you.

